# Contributing

## Setup

```sh
git clone {{ ('https://github.com/' ~ org ~ '/' ~ project_name) if org and project_name else '<repo-url>' }}
cd {{ project_name | default('<repo>', true) }}
mise install
```

`mise install` reads `.mise/conf.d/` and installs the pinned toolchain, so the
versions you build with match the versions CI builds with.
{% if contributing_hook_manager != 'none' %}
Then install the git hooks:

```sh
{% if contributing_hook_manager == 'prek' -%}
prek install
{%- elif contributing_hook_manager == 'pre-commit' -%}
pre-commit install
{%- else -%}
lefthook install
{%- endif %}
```

The hooks run on every commit. They check formatting, lint, secrets, and the
commit message, so a push that passed locally rarely fails CI on style.
{%- endif %}

## Making a change

1. Branch off the default branch.
2. Make the change, with a test that fails without it.
{%- if contributing_task_runner != 'none' %}
3. Run `{{ contributing_task_runner }} test` and `{{ contributing_task_runner }} lint`.
{%- else %}
3. Run the test suite and the linter.
{%- endif %}
4. Commit with a Conventional Commits subject.
5. Open a pull request.

## Commit messages

The commit-msg hook enforces Conventional Commits:

```
<type>[(<scope>)][!]: <description>
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`,
`chore`, `revert`. A `!` marks a breaking change.

The release tooling reads these to compute the next version and write the
changelog, so the type decides whether a change ships as a patch, a minor, or a
major.

## Pull requests

The CI gate has to pass. It is one required check that depends on every job, so a
failure names which job failed.

Keep a pull request to one concern. Two unrelated changes in one branch means a
reviewer cannot approve half of it.
